WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.767
SageMaker.

2
00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:04.752
On AWS there are two ways to run models.

3
00:00:04.752 --> 00:00:06.767
Let's see where each one fits.

4
00:00:07.967 --> 00:00:12.333
SageMaker is AWS's managed machine learning platform.

5
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:14.933
You bring a script and a model artifact.

6
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:17.200
AWS brings the hardware.

7
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.700
Here is the lifecycle.

8
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.167
You explore data in a hosted notebook.

9
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:28.400
A training job spins up GPU instances, trains, and, crucially, tears them down again.

10
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.467
The result is saved as an artifact in S3.

11
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:38.700
Then an endpoint puts your model behind an HTTPS URL with autoscaling, and no server babysitting.

12
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.433
Next to it sits Bedrock.

13
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:43.853
SageMaker is for models you train.

14
00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:46.533
Bedrock is for models someone else trained.

15
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:49.666
With SageMaker you deploy an endpoint.

16
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:53.467
With Bedrock there's one API and no GPUs to manage.

17
00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:59.100
Foundation models like Claude and Llama live there, with enterprise IAM and billing.

18
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.961
So: train it, SageMaker.

19
00:01:00.961 --> 00:01:02.067
Call it, Bedrock.

20
00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:04.967
Here is both sides in Python.

21
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:10.267
We point a model object at the artifact in S3, with a role and an inference script.

22
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:15.033
Calling deploy creates the endpoint, on the instance type and count we choose.

23
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:19.087
And on the Bedrock side, we create a client and call converse.

24
00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:20.600
No infrastructure at all.

25
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.700
Here is how it plays out for an exoplanet project.

26
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.814
The classifier trains as a SageMaker job.

27
00:01:27.814 --> 00:01:32.070
The GPU spins up, trains, writes the artifact, and spins down.

28
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.267
You pay for about forty minutes.

29
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:39.133
It then serves from an endpoint that autoscales when survey data is released.

30
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:45.546
Meanwhile the research agent's LLM calls go through Bedrock, under the same account and IAM.

31
00:01:45.546 --> 00:01:49.333
You feel exactly where your model ends and theirs begins.

32
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:51.308
Let's recap.

33
00:01:51.308 --> 00:01:54.667
SageMaker covers notebooks, training, and endpoints.

34
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:58.167
Training jobs spin GPUs up and tear them down.

35
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.600
Bedrock serves foundation models that you simply call.

36
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.297
Models you train go on SageMaker.

37
00:02:04.297 --> 00:02:06.839
Models you call go through Bedrock.

38
00:02:06.839 --> 00:02:08.800
Next up: GCP core services.
